"executive_summary": "The design of heterogeneous catalysts is hindered by the vastness of the materials space and the rarity of high-performing candidates. This study introduces a subgroup-discovery (SGD) artificial intelligence approach to identify specific physicochemical constraints that define exceptional catalytic performance in the selective oxidation of propylene. The authors synthesized and tested 120 SiO2-supported catalysts containing ruthenium, tungsten, and phosphorus using high-throughput experimentation (HTE). By combining experimental yield data with density-functional-theory (DFT) calculated free-atom properties, the SGD algorithm identified three key parameters: temperature, phosphorus content, and composition-weighted electronegativity. These parameters formed rules that exclusively described materials and conditions yielding high amounts of acrolein and acrylic acid. The identified subgroup contained only 1.2% of the data points but exhibited an average yield five times higher than the dataset average. Crucially, these rules were used to guide the design of more complex four- and five-component catalysts. Experimental validation showed that the SG rules correctly predicted high-performing compositions, with the best five-component catalyst achieving an oxygenate yield of 59.60%, more than double the maximum yield of the training set. This demonstrates that local modeling via SGD can uncover interpretable, chemically meaningful patterns that global models miss, enabling the rational design of novel, high-performance catalysts beyond the initial training space.",
